"For $f$ to be its own inverse, $f(f(x)) = x$ for all $x$.\n\nFor $x > 3$, $f(x) = k(x)$, so $f(f(x)) = k(k(x)) = x$.\n\nFor $x \\leq 3$, $f(x) = x^2 - 6x + 12$, so $f(f(x)) = f(x^2 - 6x + 12) = x$.\n\nTherefore, we have two equations:\n\n$k(k(x)) = x$ for $x > 3$,\n\n$f(x^2 - 6x + 12) = x$ for $x \\leq 3$.\n\nSolving these equations, we get:\n\n$k(x) = x$ for $x > 3$,\n\n$k(x) = x^2 - 6x + 12$ for $x \\leq 3$.\n\nThus, the function $k(x)$ is $\\boxed{x}$ for $x > 3$ and $\\boxed{x^2 - 6x + 12}$ for $x \\leq 3$."
"To solve the equation \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{\\sqrt{x-1}}=\\frac{3}{2}\\), we start by squaring both sides to eliminate the square roots:\n\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^2\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x}{x-1} = \\frac{9}{4}\n\\]\n\nNext, we solve for \\(x\\) by cross-multiplying:\n\n\\[\n4x = 9(x-1)\n\\]\n\nExpanding and simplifying:\n\n\\[\n4x = 9x - 9\n\\]\n\\[\n4x - 9x = -9\n\\]\n\\[\n-5x = -9\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{9}{5}\n\\]\n\nThus, the solution is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{9}{5}}\n\\]"
"To find the time at which the height of the ball is at its maximum, we need to determine the vertex of the parabolic path given by the equation \\( h(t) = -25t^2 + 75t + 24 \\). The vertex form of a quadratic equation \\( at^2 + bt + c \\) is given by \\( t = -\\frac{b}{2a} \\).\n\nHere, \\( a = -25 \\) and \\( b = 75 \\). Plugging these values into the formula, we get:\n\n\\[\nt = -\\frac{75}{2(-25)} = \\frac{75}{50} = 1.5\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the time at which the height of the ball is at its maximum is \\( t = 1.5 \\) seconds.\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\( 1.5 \\) seconds. I hope it is correct."
