"To solve the inequality \\( |x-2| \\leq 5.6 \\), we need to consider the definition of absolute value. The inequality \\( |x-2| \\leq 5.6 \\) means that the distance between \\( x \\) and 2 is at most 5.6. This can be written as:\n\n\\[ -5.6 \\leq x-2 \\leq 5.6 \\]\n\nNext, we solve this compound inequality. First, add 2 to all parts of the inequality:\n\n\\[ -5.6 + 2 \\leq x \\leq 5.6 + 2 \\]\n\\[ -3.6 \\leq x \\leq 7.6 \\]\n\nNow, we need to find the integers within this range. The integers between \\(-3.6\\) and \\(7.6\\) are:\n\n\\[ -3, -2, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7 \\]\n\nCounting these integers, we find there are 10 integers.\n\nTherefore, the number of integers in the solution set is \\(\\boxed{10}\\)."
