"To find the coefficient of \\(x^3\\) when the polynomials \\(24x^4 + 6x^3 + 4x^2 - 7x - 5\\) and \\(6x^3 + 3x^2 + 3x + 4\\) are multiplied, we need to identify the terms that will contribute to the \\(x^3\\) term when multiplied.\n\nThe terms that will contribute to the \\(x^3\\) term are:\n1. \\(6x^3 \\cdot 6x^3 = 36x^6\\)\n2. \\(4x^2 \\cdot 3x = 12x^3\\)\n3. \\(-7x \\cdot 4 = -28x^3\\)\n\nNow, we sum the coefficients of these terms:\n\\[\n36 + 12 - 28 = 20\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of \\(x^3\\) is \\(\\boxed{20}\\)."
"We start with the given equation:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{p} + \\frac{1}{q} = 1\n\\]\n\nFirst, we combine the fractions on the left-hand side:\n\\[\n\\frac{p+q}{pq} + \\frac{1}{pq} = 1\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\frac{p+q + 1}{pq} = 1\n\\]\n\nNext, we multiply both sides by \\( pq \\) to clear the denominator:\n\\[\np + q + 1 = pq\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the value of \\( (p-1)(q-1) \\). We expand this expression:\n\\[\n(p-1)(q-1) = pq - p - q + 1\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\( p + q + 1 = pq \\) into the expression:\n\\[\n(p-1)(q-1) = pq - (p + q + 1) + 1 = pq - pq + 1 = 0\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( (p-1)(q-1) \\) is \\(\\boxed{0}\\)."
