"In an arithmetic sequence, the difference between consecutive terms is constant. Let's denote this common difference by \\(d\\).\n\nGiven that \\(\\frac{a_4}{a_2} = 3\\), we can express \\(a_4\\) and \\(a_2\\) in terms of \\(d\\):\n\\[\na_4 = a_2 + 2d\n\\]\n\\[\na_2 = a_2 + d\n\\]\n\\[\na_4 = a_2 + 2d\n\\]\n\\[\na_5 = a_2 + 3d\n\\]\n\nWe need to find \\(\\frac{a_5}{a_3}\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{a_5}{a_3} = \\frac{a_2 + 3d}{a_2 + d} = \\frac{3}{1}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\frac{a_5}{a_3}\\) is \\(\\boxed{3}\\)."
"We know that $xy = 56$ and $x < y$. We also know that $7\\frac{1}{x} + 14\\frac{1}{y} = 4$.\n\nFirst, we can rewrite the second equation as:\n$$\\frac{7}{x} + \\frac{14}{y} = 4$$\n\nNext, we can use the first equation to express $y$ in terms of $x$:\n$$y = \\frac{56}{x}$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get:\n$$\\frac{7}{x} + \\frac{14}{\\frac{56}{x}} = 4$$\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n$$\\frac{7}{x} + \\frac{x}{4} = 4$$\n\nMultiplying through by $x$, we get:\n$$7 + x = 4x$$\n\nSolving for $x$, we get:\n$$x = \\frac{7}{3}$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $x$ is $\\boxed{\\frac{7}{3}}$."
"To determine the smallest possible integer value for \\( x \\) such that \\( f(x) = \\frac{\\sqrt{x-1}}{x - 2} \\) has a real number value, we need to ensure that the expression inside the square root is non-negative and the denominator is not zero.\n\n1. The expression inside the square root, \\( x-1 \\), must be non-negative:\n   \\[\n   x-1 \\geq 0 \\implies x \\geq 1\n   \\]\n\n2. The denominator, \\( x-2 \\), must not be zero:\n   \\[\n   x-2 \\neq 0 \\implies x \\neq 2\n   \\]\n\nCombining these conditions, we get:\n\\[\nx \\geq 1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x \\neq 2\n\\]\n\nThe smallest possible integer value for \\( x \\) that satisfies both conditions is \\( x = 1 \\).\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible integer value for \\( x \\) such that \\( f(x) \\) has a real number value is \\(\\boxed{1}\\)."
"To determine how many minutes it will take Jeremiah to travel 20 miles at a speed of 60 miles per hour, we can use the formula for time, which is:\n\n\\[ \\text{Time} = \\frac{\\text{Distance}}{\\text{Speed}} \\]\n\nHere, the distance is 20 miles and the speed is 60 miles per hour. Plugging in these values, we get:\n\n\\[ \\text{Time} = \\frac{20 \\text{ miles}}{60 \\text{ miles per hour}} = \\frac{1}{3} \\text{ hours} \\]\n\nSince there are 60 minutes in an hour, we convert the time to minutes:\n\n\\[ \\text{Time} = \\frac{1}{3} \\text{ hours} \\times 60 \\text{ minutes per hour} = 20 \\text{ minutes} \\]\n\nTherefore, it will take Jeremiah \\boxed{20} minutes to travel 20 miles."
