"For the quadratic equation $kx^2 + 10x + k = 0$ to have rational solutions, the discriminant must be a perfect square. The discriminant $\\Delta$ of the quadratic equation $kx^2 + 10x + k = 0$ is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = 10^2 - 4k\n\\]\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = 100 - 4k\n\\]\n\nFor $\\Delta$ to be a perfect square, $100 - 4k$ must be a perfect square. This implies that $4k$ must be a perfect square, or $k$ must be a perfect square.\n\nThe positive perfect squares less than or equal to 100 are $1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, 81$. Therefore, there are 9 positive integer values of $k$ for which the quadratic equation has rational solutions.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{9}\n\\]"
