"To find the point of intersection of the lines \\( s = 9 - 2t \\) and \\( t = 3s + 1 \\), we need to solve the system of equations:\n\n\\[ s = 9 - 2t \\]\n\\[ t = 3s + 1 \\]\n\nFirst, set the two expressions for \\( t \\) equal to each other:\n\n\\[ 9 - 2t = 3s + 1 \\]\n\nNext, solve for \\( s \\):\n\n\\[ 9 - 2t = 3s + 1 \\]\n\\[ 9 - 1 = 3s \\]\n\\[ 8 = 3s \\]\n\\[ s = 2 \\]\n\nNow, substitute \\( s = 2 \\) back into the first equation to find \\( t \\):\n\n\\[ t = 3(2) + 1 \\]\n\\[ t = 6 + 1 \\]\n\\[ t = 7 \\]\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is \\((2, 7)\\).\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\((2, 7)\\). hope it is correct."
