"To determine the domain of the function \\( f(x) = \\frac{1}{x-64} + \\frac{1}{x^2-64} + \\frac{1}{x^3-64} \\), we need to identify the values of \\( x \\) that make the denominators zero, as these values are not in the domain.\n\nThe denominators of the function are \\( x-64 \\), \\( x^2-64 \\), and \\( x^3-64 \\). We set each denominator equal to zero and solve for \\( x \\):\n\n1. \\( x - 64 = 0 \\Rightarrow x = 64 \\)\n2. \\( x^2 - 64 = 0 \\Rightarrow x = \\pm 8 \\)\n3. \\( x^3 - 64 = 0 \\Rightarrow x = 4 \\)\n\nThus, the values of \\( x \\) that are not denominators domain are \\( x = 64 \\), \\( x = 8 \\), and \\( x = -8 \\)."
