"To determine the degree of the polynomial \\((ax^6 + bx^8 + cx^3 + d)(x^5 + ex^4 + f)(x^2 + gx + h)\\), we need to consider the highest power of \\(x\\) that appears in the product.\n\nFirst, let's identify the degrees of each polynomial:\n- The degree of \\(ax^6 + bx^8 + cx^3 + d\\) is 6.\n- The degree of \\(x^5 + ex^4 + f\\) is 5.\n- The degree of \\(x^2 + gx + h\\) is 2.\n\nWhen multiplying these polynomials together, the degree of the resulting polynomial is the sum of the degrees of the individual polynomials. Therefore, we add the degrees of the three polynomials:\n\\[\n6 + 5 + 2 = 13\n\\]\n\nThus, the degree of the polynomial is \\(\\boxed{13}\\)."
