"We have that $4^a=5$, so $a=\\log_4(5)$. Similarly, $5^b=6$, so $b=\\log_5(6)$, $6^c=7$, so $c=\\log_6(7)$, and $7^d=8$, so $d=\\log_7(8)$.\n\nTherefore, $a\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot d=\\log_4(5)\\cdot\\log_5(6)\\cdot\\log_6(7)\\cdot\\log_7(8)$.\n\nUsing the properties of logarithms, we can simplify this expression:\n\n$$a\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot d=\\log_4(5\\cdot6\\cdot7\\cdot8)=\\log_4(1680).$$\n\nSince $4^4=16$, we have:\n\n$$a\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot d=4.$$\n\nTherefore, the answer is $\\boxed{4}$."
"We can use the formula for compound interest to find the interest earned in two years. The formula is:\n\n\\[ A = P(1 + r)^n \\]\n\nwhere \\( A \\) is the amount after \\( n \\) years, \\( P \\) is the principal amount, \\( r \\) is the annual interest rate, and \\( n \\) is the number of years.\n\nGiven:\n- \\( P = 10000 \\)\n- \\( r = 0.045 \\)\n- \\( n = 2 \\)\n\nFirst, we calculate the amount after two years:\n\n\\[ A = 10000(1 + 0.045)^2 = 10000 \\times 1.0901 = 10901 \\]\n\nThe interest earned is the amount after the years minus the principal:\n\n\\[ \\text{Interest} = A - P = 10901 - 10000 = 901 \\]\n\nTherefore, the interest earned in two years is \\(\\boxed{901}\\)."
