"Let's denote the value of a triangle as \\(T\\) and the value of a circle as \\(C\\).\n\nFrom the first equation, we have:\n\\[8T + 4C = 21\\]\n\nFrom the second equation, we have:\n\\[4T + 8C = 16\\]\n\nWe can simplify the second equation by dividing all terms by 4:\n\\[T + 2C = 4\\]\n\nNow, we have a system of linear equations:\n\\[8T + 4C = 21\\]\n\\[T + 2C = 4\\]\n\nWe can solve this system by subtracting the second equation from the first:\n\\[(8T + 4C) - (T + 2C) = 21 - 4\\]\n\\[7T + 2C = 17\\]\n\\[T + C = \\frac{17}{7}\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of two circles is:\n\\[\\boxed{\\frac{17}{7}}\\]"
"For the quadratic equation \\(x^2 + 6mx + m\\), to have exactly one real root, the discriminant must be zero. The discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of a quadratic equation \\(ax^2 + bx + c\\) is given by \\(\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\\).\n\nIn this case, \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = 6m\\), and \\(c = m\\). Therefore, the discriminant is:\n\\[\n\\Delta = (6m)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot m = 36m^2 - 4m^2 = 32m^2\n\\]\n\nFor the quadratic to have exactly one real root, the discriminant must be zero:\n\\[\n32m^2 = 0\n\\] m^2 = 0 \\Rightarrow m = 0\n\\]\n\nHowever, the problem asks for the positive value of \\(m\\). Therefore, the positive value of \\(m\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]"
