"To determine the probability that flipping a fair coin three times produces fewer than two heads, we need to consider all possible outcomes and count the favorable ones.\n\nFirst, let's list all possible outcomes when flipping a fair coin three times:\n1. HHH\n2. HHT\n3. HTH\n4. THH\n5. THT\n6. TTH\n7. TTT\n\nThere are 8 possible outcomes in total.\n\nNext, we count the favorable outcomes where there are fewer than two heads:\n1. HHT\n2. HTH\n3. THH\n4. THT\n5. TTH\n\nThere are 5 favorable outcomes.\n\nThe probability of an event is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of possible outcomes. Therefore, the probability that flipping a fair coin three times produces fewer than two heads is:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\text{Number of favorable outcomes}}{\\text{Total number of possible outcomes}} = \\frac{5}{8}\n\\]\n\nThus, the probability is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{5}{8}}\\)."
