"To determine the number of 3-digit descending numbers, we need to consider the constraints on the digits of the number. Each digit must be smaller than the digit that comes before it.\n\n1. The hundreds digit can be any digit from 1 to 9. This gives us 9 possible choices.\n2. The tens digit must be smaller than the hundreds digit. Therefore, it can be any digit from 0 to 8. This gives us 9 possible choices.\n3. The units digit must be smaller than both the hundreds and tens digits. Therefore, it can be any digit from 0 to 7. This gives us 8 possible choices.\n\nTo find the total number of 3-digit descending numbers, we multiply the number of choices for each digit:\n\n\\[\n9 \\text{ (choices for hundreds digit)} \\times 9 \\text{ (choices for tens digit)} \\times 8 \\text{ (choices for units digit)} = 648\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of  3-digit descending numbers is \\(\\boxed{648}\\)."
