"To determine how much Minh would pay for four dozen doughnuts, we first need to find the cost of one donut. We know that Minh paid \\$3 for four doughnuts. Therefore, the cost of one donut is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Cost of one donut} = \\frac{\\$3}{4 \\text{ dough}}}} = \\$0.75 \\text{ per donut}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the cost of four dozen doughnuts. Since one dozen equals 12 doughnuts, four dozen equals:\n\n\\[\n4 \\text{ dozen} \\times 12 \\text{ doughnuts per dozen} = 48 \\text{ doughnuts}\n\\]\n\nNow, we multiply the cost of one donut by the total number of doughnuts:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Total cost} = 48 \\text{ doughnuts} \\times \\$0.75 \\text{ per donut} = \\$36\n\\]\n\nTherefore, Minh would pay \\boxed{36} dollars for four dozen doughnuts."
