"To find the value of \\( x \\) in the given triangle, we need to use the fact that the sum of the angles in a triangle is always \\( 180^\\circ \\).\n\nGiven:\n- One angle is \\( 60^\\circ \\).\n- Another angle is \\( 50^\\circ \\).\n- The third angle is \\( x^\\circ \\).\n\nThe sum of the angles in the triangle is:\n\\[ 60^\\circ + 50^\\circ + x^\\circ = 180^\\circ \\]\n\nSubstituting the known angles:\n\\[ 110^\\circ + x^\\circ = 180^\\circ \\]\n\nSolving for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x^\\circ = 180^\\circ - 110^\\circ \\]\n\\[ x^\\circ = 70^\\circ \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( x \\) is \\(\\boxed{70}\\)."
"To find the length of \\( AC \\), we need to determine the distance between points \\( A \\) and \\( C \\). Given that \\( OC = 32 \\) and \\( CB = 36 \\), we can use the Pythagorean theorem to find \\( AC \\).\n\nFirst, we apply the Pythagorean theorem to the triangle \\( OCB \\):\n\\[\nAC^2 = OC^2 + CB^2\n\\]\nSubstituting the given values:\n\\[\nAC^2 = 32^2 + 36^2\n\\]\n\\[\nAC^2 = 1024 + 1296\n\\]\n\\[\nAC^2 = 2320\n\\]\nTaking the square root of both sides:\n\\[\nAC = \\sqrt{2320}\n\\]\n\\[\nAC = 48\n\\]\n\nThus, the length of \\( AC \\) is \\(\\boxed{48}\\)."
