"To solve this problem, we can use the principle of inclusion-exclusion. Let's denote the number of 4-door and red cars as \\( x \\).\n\nGiven:\n- Total number of cars = 20\n- Number of red cars = 12\n- Number of 4-door cars = 15\n- Number of 2-door and white cars = 4\n\nWe need to find the number of 4-door and red cars, which is \\( x \\).\n\nUsing the principle of inclusion-exclusion:\n\\[ \\text{Total number of cars} = \\text{Number of red cars} + \\text{Number of 4-door cars} - \\text{Number of 2 and white cars} + \\text{Number of 4 and red cars} \\]\n\nPlugging in the given values:\n\\[ 20 = 12 + 15 - 4 + x \\]\n\\[ 20 = 23 + x \\]\n\\[ x = 3 \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of 4-door and red cars is \\(\\boxed{3}\\)."
