"To determine the probability that a prime number faces up on a fair 6-sided die roll, we first need to identify the prime numbers on the die. The numbers on a 6-sided die are 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6. The prime numbers among these numbers are 2, 3, and 5.\n\nThere are 3 prime numbers on the die. The total number of possible outcomes when rolling a 6-sided die is 6.\n\nThe probability \\( P \\) of rolling a prime number is given by the ratio of the number of favorable outcomes to the total number of possible outcomes:\n\n\\[ P(\\text{prime number}) = \\frac{\\text{Number of prime numbers}}{\\text{Total number of outcomes}} = \\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2} \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that a prime number faces up when a fair 6-sided die is rolled is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}}\\)."
