"To determine how many quarters are needed to make a stack one foot high, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Convert the height of the stack from feet to inches.\n2. Determine how many quarters are needed to reach that height.\n\nFirst, we know that 1 foot is equal to 12 inches.\n\nNext, we know that a stack of eight quarters is exactly half an inch. Therefore, each quarter is \\(\\frac{1}{8}\\) of an inch thick.\n\nTo find out how many quarters are needed to make a stack 12 inches high, we divide the total height in inches by the thickness of one quarter:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Number of quarters} = \\frac{12 \\text{ inches}}{\\frac{1}{8} \\text{ inch/quarter}} = 12 \\times 8 = 96\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of quarters needed to make a stack one foot high is \\(\\boxed{96}\\)."
"To simplify \\(\\frac{64}{7} \\div \\frac{8}{3}\\), we can use the property of division of fractions, which states that dividing by a fraction is equivalent to multiplying by its reciprocal. Therefore, we can rewrite the expression as:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{64}{7} \\div \\frac{8}{3} = \\frac{64}{7} \\times \\frac{3}{8}\n\\]\n\nNext, we multiply the numerators together and the denominators together:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{64 \\times 3}{7 \\times 8} = \\frac{192}{56}\n\\]\n\nNow, we simplify the fraction by finding the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 192 and 56. The GCD of 192 and 56 is 8. We divide both the numerator and the denominator by their GCD:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{192 \\div 8}{56 \\div 8} = \\frac{24}{7}\n\\]\n\nThus, the simplified form is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{8}{7}}\n\\]"
