"To simplify \\(\\frac{100}{180}\\), we first find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 100 and 180.\n\nThe prime factorization of 100 is \\(2^2 \\times 5^2\\).\nThe prime factorization of 180 is \\(2^2 \\times 3^2 5\\).\n\nThe GCD is the product of the lowest powers of all common prime factors, which is \\(2^2 \\times 5 = 20\\).\n\nNow, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by their GCD:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{100 \\div 20}{180 \\div 20} = \\frac{5}{9}\n\\]\n\nThus, the simplified form of \\(\\frac{100}{180}\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{5}{9}}\\)."
"To find the probability that the red die shows a 2 and the green die shows a 5, we need to consider the total number of possible outcomes and the number of favorable outcomes.\n\nEach die has 6 faces, so the total number of possible outcomes when rolling two dice is:\n\\[ 6 \\times 6 = 36 \\]\n\nNext, we determine the number of favorable outcomes where the red die shows a 2 and the green die shows a 5. There is only one such combination:\n\\[ (2, 5) \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability \\( P \\) of this specific combination occurring is:\n\\[ P = \\frac{\\text{Number of favorable outcomes}}{\\text{Total number of possible outcomes}} = \\frac{1}{36} \\]\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(\\frac{1}{36}\\). I hope it is correct."
