"To find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of \\(6^3\\) and \\(3^6\\), we start by expressing both numbers in terms of their prime factors.\n\nFirst, express \\(6^3\\):\n\\[6^3 = (2 \\cdot 3^3)^3 = 2^3 \\cdot 3^9.\\]\n\nNext, express \\(3^6\\):\n\\[3^6 = 3^6.\\]\n\nThe GCD is the product of the lowest powers of all common prime factors. Here, the common prime factor is \\(3\\), and the lowest power of \\(3\\) in both factorizations is \\(3^6\\).\n\nThus, the GCD is:\n\\[3^6 = 729.\\]\n\nTherefore, the greatest common divisor of \\(6^3\\) and \\(3^6\\) is \\(\\boxed{729}\\)."
"To find the area of the circular emblem, we need to determine the side length of the square tarp and then use that to find the radius of the circular emblem.\n\nThe area of the square tarp is given as 196 square feet. Since the area of a square is the square of its side length, we can find the side length of the square by taking the square root of 196:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{196} = 14\n\\]\n\nThus, the side length of the square tarp is 14 feet. Since the circular emblem is completely covered by the smallest possible square tarp, the radius of the circular emblem is equal to the side length of the square tarp, which is 14 feet.\n\nThe area of a circle is given by the formula \\(\\pi r^2\\). Therefore, the radius of the circular emblem is:\n\n\\[\n\\pi (14)^2 = 196\\pi\n\\]\n\nThus, the area of the circular emblem is \\(\\boxed{196\\pi}\\) square feet."
