"To find the least common multiple (LCM) of $14$, $20$, and $35$, we first find the prime factorizations of each number:\n\n- $14 = 2 \\cdot 7$\n- $20 = 2^2 \\cdot 5$\n- $35 = 5 \\cdot 7$\n\nThe LCM is found by taking the highest power of each prime that appears in the factorizations:\n\n- The highest power of $2$ is $2^2$ (from $20$).\n- The highest power of $5$ is $5$ (from $20$ and $35$).\n- The highest power of $7$ is $7$ (from $14$ and $35$).\n\nThus, the LCM is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{LCM} = 2^2 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 7 = 4 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 7 = 140\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the least common multiple of $14$, $20$, and $35$ is $\\boxed{140}$. I hope it is correct."
