"We know that the least common multiple (LCM) of two integers is equal to their greatest common divisor (GCD) multiplied by the product of the two integers. Let the two integers be \\(a\\) and \\(b\\). Then, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\text{LCM}(a, b) \\times \\text{GCD}(a, b) = a \\times b.\n\\]\n\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\text{LCM}(a, b) = 240,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{GCD}(a, b) = 24,\n\\]\n\\[\na = 48.\n\\]\n\nWe need to find \\(b\\). Using the relationship:\n\n\\[\n240 \\times 24 = 48 \\times b.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n5760 = 48 \\times b.\n\\]\n\n\\[\nb = \\frac{5760}{48}.\n\\]\n\n\\[\nb = 120.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the other integer is \\(\\boxed{120}\\)."
