"To determine the probability that a randomly selected number from 1 to 100 is a perfect square, we first need to identify the perfect squares within this range.\n\nThe perfect squares between 1 and 100 are:\n\\[ 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, 81, 100 \\]\n\nThere are 10 perfect squares in this range.\n\nThe probability \\( P \\) of selecting a perfect square is given by the ratio of the number of perfect squares to the total number of possible outcomes:\n\n\\[ P = \\frac{\\text{Number of perfect squares}}{\\text{Total number of outcomes}} = \\frac{10}{100} = \\frac{1}{10} \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that the number is a perfect square is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{10}}\\)."
