"The particle travels along the line \\( y = \\frac{3}{2} x - 2 \\). At time \\( t = 0 \\), it starts at the point \\((-2, -5)\\). The particle moves so that its \\( x \\)-coordinate changes at a rate of 4 units per unit of time.\n\nTo find the \\( y \\)-coordinate of the particle as a function of \\( t \\), we start with the equation of the line:\n\\[ y = \\frac{3}{2} x - 2 \\]\n\nSince the \\( x \\)-coordinate changes at a rate of 4 units per unit of time, we can substitute \\( x = 4t \\) into the equation of the line:\n\\[ y = \\frac{3}{2} (4t) - 2 \\]\n\\[ y = 6t - 2 \\]\n\nTherefore, the \\( y \\)-coordinate of the particle as a function of \\( t \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{6t - 2} \\]"
"We can rewrite the expression as\n\n$$\\frac{\\sec^4 \\alpha}{\\tan^2 \\beta} + \\frac{\\sec^4 \\beta}{\\tan^2 \\alpha} = \\frac{1}{\\tan^2 \\beta} + \\frac{1}{\\tan^2 \\alpha}.$$\n\nLet $x = \\tan^2 \\alpha$ and $y = \\tan^2 \\beta$. Then the expression becomes\n\n$$\\frac{1}{x} + \\frac{1}{y}.$$\n\nWe know that $x + y = 1$ and $xy = 0$. Therefore, the expression becomes\n\n$$\\frac{1}{x} + \\frac{1}{y} = \\frac{1}{0} + \\frac{1}{1} = \\boxed{2}.$$\n\n​"
