"We know that $\\mathbf{c} \\cdot (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}) = (\\mathbf{c} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}) \\cos \\theta,$ where $\\theta$ is the angle between $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf{c} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}:$\n\\[\n\\mathbf{c} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = (2)(-1) + (-7)(4) + (-10)(6) = -2 - 28 - 60 = -90.\n\\]\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}$ is a unit vector, $\\cos \\theta = 1.$ Therefore, the maximum value of $\\mathbf{c} \\cdot (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b})$ is\n\\[\n(\\mathbf{c} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}) \\cos \\theta = (-90) \\cdot 1 = \\boxed{-90}.\n\\]"
