"To find the matrix that corresponds to reflecting over the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we need to use the fact that reflection over a vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} a \\\\ b \\end{pmatrix}\\) can be represented by the matrix:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n1 & -a \\\\\n-b & 1\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nHere, the vector is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\), so \\(a = -1\\) and \\(b = 3\\). Substituting these values into the matrix, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n1 & -(-1) \\\\\n-3 & 1\n\\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix}\n1 & 1 \\\\\n-3 & 1\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThus, the matrix that corresponds to reflecting over the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix}\n1 & 1 \\\\\n-3 & 1\n\\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]"
"To find the matrix $\\mathbf{M}$ that swaps the rows of a matrix, we need to find a matrix $\\mathbf{M}$ such that:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} c & d \\\\ a & b \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThis means that $\\mathbf{M}$ should be a matrix that, when multiplied by any $2 \\times 2$ matrix, swaps its rows. The only matrix that satisfies this property is the swap matrix, which is defined as:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThus, the matrix $\\mathbf{M}$ that swaps the rows of a matrix is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]"
